From https://x.com/__nmca__/status/1870170101091008860:

> o1 was the first large reasoning model — as we outlined in the original “Learning to Reason” blog, it’s “just” an LLM trained with RL. o3 is powered by further scaling up RL beyond o1

@ryan_greenblatt Shouldn't this be interpreted as a very big update vs. the neuralese-in-o3 hypothesis?

contents.markdown:
> A fairer comparison would probably be to actually try hard at building the kind of scaffold which could use ~10k$ in inference costs productively. I suspect the resulting agent would probably not do much better than with 100$ of inference, but it seems hard to be confident. And it seems harder still to be confident about what will happen even in just 3 years' time, given that [pretraining compute seems like it will probably grow about 10x/year](https://x.com/peterwildeford/status/1825614599623782490) and that there might be [stronger pushes towards automated ML](https://x.com/RobertTLange/status/1829104918214447216).

A related announcement, explicitly targeting 'building an epistemically sound research agent   
@elicitorg that can use unlimited test-time compute while keeping reasoning transparent & verifiable': [https://x.com/stuhlmueller/status/1869080354658890009](https://x.com/stuhlmueller/status/1869080354658890009).

